etails and comments thoroughly.\\n   - When you see screenshots or images in issue descriptions, pull request descriptions, comments, or discussions, download the image to a local file first, then use the Read tool to view and analyze it. Before reading downloaded images with the Read tool, verify that the file is a valid image rather than HTML by using a CLI tool such as the 'file' command. When the file command shows \\\"HTML\\\", \\\"text\\\", or \\\"ASCII text\\\", the download failed, so do not call Read on that file. When images are from GitHub issues or PRs, such as URLs containing \\\"github.com/user-attachments\\\", use: curl -L -H \\\"Authorization: token $(gh auth token)\\\" -o <filename> \\\"<url>\\\"\\n   - When you need issue details, use gh issue view https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4082.\\n   - When you need related code, use gh search code --owner link-assistant [keywords].\\n   - When you need repo context, read files in your working directory.\\n   - When you study related work, study the most recent related pull requests.\\n   - When the issue is not defined clearly enough, write a comment with clarifying questions.\\n   - When accessing GitHub Gists, use gh gist view command instead of direct URL fetching.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug, find the actual root cause first and run as many experiments as needed.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug and the code does not have enough tracing or logs, add them and keep them in the code with the default state switched off.\\n   - When you need comments on a pull request, note that GitHub has three different comment types with different API endpoints:\\n      1. PR review comments (inline code comments): gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/pulls/4083/comments --paginate\\n      2. PR conversation comments (general discussion): gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4083/comments --paginate\\n      3. PR reviews (approve/request changes): gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/pulls/4083/reviews --paginate\\n      Note: The command \\\"gh pr view --json comments\\\" only returns conversation comments and misses review comments.\\n   - When you need the latest comments on the issue, use gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4082/comments --paginate.\\nSolution development and testing.\\n   - When coding, commit each atomic step that is useful on its own to the pull request branch so interrupted work remains preserved in the pull request.\\n   - When implementing changes, prefer drafting code for all requirements before starting verification, then use focused checks and existing CI/CD coverage.\\n   - When validating changes, prefer a single relevant test or small focused checks; check whether the command triggers a full source build before running it.\\n   - When CI/CD already covers full test suites or CPU-intensive builds, prefer leaving those to CI/CD and checking results for the current commit; avoid duplicating them locally unless user requirements or uncovered failures call for it.\\n   - When CI/CD coverage is missing and adding it was not requested, suggest https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/blob/main/docs/CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md; let the repository owner decide whether to add it, and meanwhile complete required validation with available resources.\\n   - When writing tests, prefer small cases with mocks and the existing integration framework.\\n   - When you test solution draft, include automated checks in pr.\\n   - When you write or modify tests, consider setting reasonable timeouts at test, suite, and CI job levels so failures surface quickly instead of hanging.\\n   - When you see repeated test timeout patterns in CI, investigate the root cause rather than increasing timeouts.\\n   - When the issue is unclear, write a comment on the issue with questions.\\n   - When you encounter any problems that you are unable to solve yourself, write a comment to the pull request asking for help.\\n   - When you need human help, use gh pr comment 4083 --body \\\"your message\\\" to comment on existing PR.\\nReproducible testing.\\n   - When fixing a bug, prefer a minimal regression test that fails before the fix and passes afterward.\\n   - When encountering UI bugs, capture a screenshot showing the problem state, then create a visual regression test or manual verification screenshot after the fix.\\n   - When submitting a fix, include in the PR description: (1) how to reproduce the issue, (2) the automated test that verifies the fix, (3) before/after screenshots for UI issues.\\n   - When a bug fix does not have a reproducing test, treat the fix as incomplete because regressions can occur later without notice.\\nPreparing pull request.\\n   - When you code, follow contributing guidelines.\\n   - When you commit, write clear message.\\n   - When you need examples of style, use gh pr list --repo link-assistant/hive-mind --state merged --search [keywords].\\n   - When you open pr, describe solution draft and include tests.\\n   - When there is a package with version and GitHub Actions workflows for automatic release, update the version in your pull request to prepare for next release.\\n   - When you update existing pr 4083, use gh pr edit to modify title and description.\\n   - When you finalize the pull request:\\n      check that the pull request title and description are updated (the PR may start with a [WIP] prefix and a placeholder description that should be replaced with the actual title and description of the changes),\\n      follow style from merged prs for code, title, and description,\\n      check that no uncommitted changes corresponding to the original requirements are left behind,\\n      check that the default branch is merged into the pull request branch,\\n      check that all CI checks are passing if they exist before you finish, including checks that already fail on the default branch (when a check needs a human action, make CI on the pull request pass without it and CI on the default branch fail with an explicit message saying what to do),\\n      check for latest comments on the issue and pull request to ensure no recent feedback was missed,\\n      double-check that all changes in the pull request address the original requirements of the issue,\\n      check for newly introduced bugs in the pull request by carefully reading gh pr diff,\\n      check that no previously existing features were removed without an explicit request in the issue description, issue comments, or pull request comments.\\n   - When you finish implementation, use gh pr ready 4083.\\nWorkflow and collaboration.\\n   - When you check branch, verify with git branch --show-current.\\n   - When you push, push only to branch issue-4082-6562da9f0cb2.\\n   - When you finish, create a pull request from branch issue-4082-6562da9f0cb2.\\n   - When pr 4083 already exists for this branch, update it instead of creating new one.\\n   - When you organize workflow, use pull requests instead of direct merges to default branch (main or master).\\n   - When you manage commits, preserve commit history for later analysis.\\n   - When you contribute, keep repository history forward-moving with regular commits, pushes, and reverts if needed.\\n   - When you face conflict that you cannot resolve yourself, ask for help.\\n   - When you collaborate, respect branch protections by working only on issue-4082-6562da9f0cb2.\\n   - When you mention a result, include the pull request URL or comment URL.\\nSelf review.\\n   - When you check your solution draft, verify git status shows a clean working tree with no uncommitted changes.\\n   - When you compare with repo style, use gh pr diff [number].\\n   - When you finalize, confirm code, tests, and description are consistent.\\nGitHub CLI command patterns.\\n   - When fetching lists from GitHub API, use the --paginate flag to ensure all results are returned (GitHub returns max 30 per page by default).\\n   - When adding PR comment, use gh pr comment NUMBER --body \\\"text\\\" --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When adding issue comment, use gh issue comment NUMBER --body \\\"text\\\" --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When viewing PR details, use gh pr view NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When filtering with jq, use gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/pulls/4083/comments --paginate --jq 'reverse | .[0:5]'.\\nPlaywright MCP usage (browser automation via MCP tools).\\n   - When you develop frontend web applications (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, Vue, Angular, etc.), use Playwright MCP tools to test the UI in a real browser.\\n   - When WebFetch tool fails to retrieve expected content (e.g., returns empty content, JavaScript-rendered pages, or login-protected pages), use Playwright MCP tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot) as a fallback for web browsing.\\n   - When WebSearch tool fails or returns insufficient results, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot) as a fallback for internet search.\\n   - When you need to interact with dynamic web pages that require JavaScript execution, use Playwright MCP tools.\\n   - When you need to visually verify how a web page looks or take screenshots, use browser_take_screenshot from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you need to fill forms, click buttons, or perform user interactions on web pages, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_click, browser_type, browser_fill_form).\\n   - When you need to test responsive design or different viewport sizes, use browser_resize from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you finish using the browser, close it with browser_close to free resources.\\n   - When reproducing UI bugs, use browser_take_screenshot to capture the problem state before implementing any fix.\\n   - When fixing UI bugs, take before/after screenshots to provide visual evidence of the fix for human verification.\\n   - When creating UI tests, save baseline screenshots to the repository for visual regression testing.\\n   - When verifying UI fixes, compare screenshots to ensure the fix does not introduce unintended visual changes.\\nIssue completion goal.\\n   - Do not stop until every requirement of the issue and all required sub-issues is fully implemented and verified in this single pull request, in the widest requested scope, with no requested work deferred; work that is truly blocked must be reported as blocked and left unfinished, never presented as done.\\n   - Try to set this exact objective with a native goal-setting API or tool before working, and keep it active until every acceptance criterion is verified. Codex exposes thread/goal/set through its app-server API when available; do not invent an exec --goal flag. If the running tool provides no native goal API, keep the objective and checklist in your work plan across turns and compaction.\\n   - Read the complete issue, every comment, every required sub-issue, and all pull request conversation comments, inline comments, and reviews. Inventory each requirement, including prose requirements and later feedback, and map each one to implementation and verification evidence.\\n   - Passing CI, a partial implementation, an upstream report, or a workaround does not complete an unmet acceptance criterion. Continue authorized work. If an external blocker cannot be resolved, report the exact blocked criteria and evidence, leave them unfinished, and keep the pull request unmerged.\\n   - Preserve a positive closing keyword in the pull request description for the original issue and every required issue. Repeat the keyword per issue, use the correct repository for foreign issues, and keep references outside examples or code blocks. A negated sentence (for example \\\"does not close\\\" before an issue reference) is not a closing reference. Mention issues you reported to other repositories without a keyword: GitHub also closes an issue linked right after \\\"fix:\\\", as in \\\"a suggested fix: <issue URL>\\\".\\nLocation privacy: Keep the user's and host's geolocation private. Avoid collecting, inferring, or transmitting their location, including IP-geolocation lookups, GPS/browser location permissions, local timezone or region probes, and user_location hints in search or model requests. Use location-neutral searches. Propagate this policy to delegated agents and browser/MCP tools. Locations explicitly present in task content may be used to discuss that content; they do not authorize discovery or disclosure of the user's or host's location.\\nIssue to solve: https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4082\\nYour prepared branch: issue-4082-6562da9f0cb2\\nYour prepared working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794991263584\\nYour prepared Pull Request: https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/pull/4083\\nCollect data related about the issue to this repository, make sure we compile that data into the ./dev/log/issues/4082/pulls/4083 folder.\\nDo not stop until every requirement of the issue and all required sub-issues is fully implemented and verified in this single pull request, in the widest requested scope, with no requested work deferred; work that is truly blocked must be reported as blocked and left unfinished, never presented as done.\\nProceed.\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me run the requested command to get that for you.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_bc1338171e1eabe8\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"command\\\":\\\"gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4082' --json title --jq .title && echo && gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4082' --json body --jq .body; printf '\\\\n__formal_ai_exit=%s\\\\n' $?\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_bc1338171e1eabe8\",\"content\":\"Collected operational cases: review findings and reviews after \\\"Ready to merge\\\" never auto-addressed, stale-feedback window, unresolved threads, merge-order prerequisites, missing corepack in the task image\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nThis collects review-handling and environment cases seen while operating Hive Mind (solve 2.39.x, Claude tool, docker isolation, `--auto-restart-until-mergeable`, `--on-session-kill=resume`). Each one needed a human to step in. They are anonymized. The main feature request (review-bot awareness and auto-restart on review findings) is filed separately as #4081. This issue lists every distinct case, so each can be checked off.\\n\\n## Cases\\n\\n### 1. \\\"Ready to merge\\\" posted after a bot review finding arrived\\n\\n**Timeline:**\\n- The session ends and marks the PR ready.\\n- About 90 s later, a review app posts an inline P1/P2 finding.\\n- About 4 min later, Hive Mind posts \\\"✅ Ready to merge\\\".\\n\\nAccording to the code on `main`, the restart loop sees such a comment and ignores it as `[bot]` (`checkForNonBotComments`). This was the most frequent case. Sometimes the finding came seconds *before* the session summary, and it was still not addressed.\\n\\n**Expected:** restart with the finding as feedback. See #4081.\\n\\n### 2. Bot re-review on a later push, after the loop had exited\\n\\n**Timeline:**\\n- The maintainer asks for follow-up work.\\n- A session pushes and posts \\\"Ready to merge\\\".\\n- About 1 h later, the review app reviews the new head and posts a new P2 finding.\\n\\nNo session started. The loop had already exited after \\\"Ready to merge\\\", and the finding sat unaddressed until a human asked.\\n\\n**Expected:** a watch or resume that is triggered by new reviews.\\n\\n### 3. Human review after \\\"Ready to merge\\\" needed a manual restart every time\\n\\n**Pattern:**\\n- A reviewer posts a detailed review (BLOCK / changes needed) as a PR conversation comment after \\\"Ready to merge\\\".\\n- The maintainer then has to comment \\\"this review must be fully addressed\\\" to start a session.\\n\\nThis repeated 4–5 times on one PR. In several cases the reviewer posted through the **same GitHub account** Hive Mind uses. The loop trusts same-account comments only while it runs (#1821), and after exiting it does not run at all.\\n\\n**Expected:** auto-resume on new non-tool comments and reviews after \\\"Ready to merge\\\". Same-account comments without tool markers should count as human feedback.\\n\\n### 4. Findings older than the last commit fall out of session-start feedback\\n\\n`src/solve.feedback.lib.mjs` counts PR review comments only when `created_at > lastCommitTime` (and `CHANGES_REQUESTED` reviews likewise). Suppose a session pushes any commit after a bot finding without addressing it, for example a merge of the default branch. The next session's prompt no longer mentions that finding, although its thread is still unresolved.\\n\\n**Expected:** always list unresolved review threads (GraphQL `reviewThreads { isResolved }`), regardless of age.\\n\\n### 5. Threads replied to but never resolved\\n\\nThe AI replies \\\"Fixed in `<sha>` …\\\" to a review thread, which is correct, but leaves the thread unresolved. Several merged PRs ended with every review thread unresolved even though each had a fix reply. So thread state cannot be used as a \\\"findings addressed\\\" signal. Reviewers also see a long list of open conversations.\\n\\n**Expected:** after a verified fix, the AI (or the tool) resolves the thread. The ready gate counts only threads that are still unresolved.\\n\\n### 6. \\\"Ready to merge\\\" despite a merge-order prerequisite stated by the session itself\\n\\nThe session summary said \\\"merge the companion PR in the other repository first\\\". Hive Mind then posted \\\"✅ Ready to merge\\\" with only \\\"CI passed, no conflicts, no uncommitted changes\\\". A reader of the final status comment would merge in the wrong order.\\n\\n**Expected:** \\\"Ready to merge\\\" either names unresolved prerequisites that the AI declared (or a \\\"Depends on\\\" link in the PR body), or is replaced by \\\"Ready for review — blocked by …\\\". Related: #3924, #3911.\\n\\n### 7. Pre-commit hooks fail in the task image: `spawnSync corepack ENOENT`\\n\\nThe task image runs Node 26 (`HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION=26.13.1`), and inside it `command -v corepack` finds nothing: Node ≥ 25 no longer bundles `corepack`, and the Dockerfile explicitly skips re-installing inherited `corepack@*` globals. Repositories whose git hooks run the package manager through corepack fail on every commit. This includes lefthook with `packageManager: pnpm@…`:\\n\\n```\\nlefthook runner: failed to start corepack: spawnSync corepack ENOENT\\nexit status 127\\n```\\n\\nSo the AI has to bypass the hooks (`LEFTHOOK=0` / `--no-verify`) and run the formatters and linters by hand. If it doesn't, the checks the hooks would have run are silently skipped.\\n\\n**Expected:** install `corepack` globally in the image (`npm i -g corepack`), or provide `pnpm`/`yarn` shims. Also tell the AI when hooks cannot run.\\n\\n### 8. OOM notice after \\\"Ready to merge\\\"\\n\\nA process in the task cgroup (≈2.9 GB limit) was OOM-killed early in the session, for example a type-check run at the default V8 heap. The session itself completed. A \\\"container OOM event\\\" notice was then posted *after* \\\"Ready to merge\\\". This is already tracked in #2809 and #2838. It is listed here only so the set of cases is complete.\\n\\n## Suggested acceptance\\n\\nEach case above has a regression test, or is closed as a duplicate with a link. Cases 1–5 can share the review-detection work in #4081.\\n\\n\\n__formal_ai_exit=0\\n\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me open /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794991263584/src/solve.feedback.lib.mjs and read what it says.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_d4228eb1d5f9a77e\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"read\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794991263584/src/solve.feedback.lib.mjs\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_d4228eb1d5f9a77e\",\"content\":\"<file>\\n00001| import { promptText } from './prompt-text.lib.mjs';\\n00002| /**\\n00003|  * Feedback detection module for solve.mjs\\n00004|  * Handles comment counting and feedback detection for continue mode\\n00005|  */\\n00006| \\n00007| // Import Sentry integration\\n00008| import { reportError } from './sentry.lib.mjs';\\n00009| \\n00010| import { wrapDollarWithGhRetry as _wrapDollarWithGhRetry } from './github-rate-limit.lib.mjs'; // rate-limit marker (#1726): gh API calls flow through $ wrapped by caller\\n00011| import { QUIET_PROBE, quietProbe } from './quiet-probe.lib.mjs'; // issue #2130: keep read-only probe payloads out of the attached log\\n00012| // Issue #1827: tool-generated comments (markers + in-memory tracked IDs) must\\n00013| // not count as feedback in watch/continue mode, mirroring checkForNonBotComments.\\n00014| import { isToolGeneratedComment, isToolTrackedCommentId } from './tool-comments.lib.mjs';\\n00015| import { detectExternalContentEdits, formatEditEvidence } from './description-edits.lib.mjs'; // issue #2293\\n00016| export const detectAndCountFeedback = async params => {\\n00017|   const { prNumber, branchName, owner, repo, issueNumber, isContinueMode, argv, mergeStateStatus, prState, workStartTime, log, formatAligned, cleanErrorMessage, $, repositoryPath = null } = params;\\n00018| \\n00019|   let newPrComments = 0;\\n00020|   let newPrReviewComments = 0;\\n00021|   let newIssueComments = 0;\\n00022|   let commentInfo = '';\\n00023|   let feedbackLines = [];\\n00024|   let currentUser = null;\\n00025| \\n00026|   // Get current GitHub user to filter out own comments\\n00027|   try {\\n00028|     const userResult = await quietProbe($)`gh api user --jq .login`;\\n00029|     if (userResult.code === 0) {\\n00030|       currentUser = userResult.stdout.toString().trim();\\n00031|       await log(formatAligned('👤', 'Current user:', currentUser, 2));\\n00032|     }\\n00033|   } catch (error) {\\n00034|     reportError(error, {\\n00035|       context: 'get_current_user',\\n00036|       operation: 'gh_api_user',\\n00037|     });\\n00038|     await log('Warning: Could not get current GitHub user', { level: 'warning' });\\n00039|   }\\n00040| \\n00041|   // Debug logging to understand when comment counting doesn't run\\n00042|   if (argv.verbose) {\\n00043|     await log('\\\\n📊 Comment counting conditions:', { verbose: true });\\n00044|     await log(`   prNumber: ${prNumber || 'NOT SET'}`, { verbose: true });\\n00045|     await log(`   branchName: ${branchName || 'NOT SET'}`, { verbose: true });\\n00046|     await log(`   isContinueMode: ${isContinueMode}`, { verbose: true });\\n00047|     await log(`   Will count comments: ${!!(prNumber && branchName)}`, { verbose: true });\\n00048|     if (!prNumber) {\\n00049|       await log('   ⚠️  Skipping: prNumber not set', { verbose: true });\\n00050|     }\\n00051|     if (!branchName) {\\n00052|       await log('   ⚠️  Skipping: branchName not set', { verbose: true });\\n00053|     }\\n00054|   }\\n00055| \\n00056|   if (prNumber && branchName) {\\n00057|     try {\\n00058|       await log(`${formatAligned('💬', 'Counting comments:', 'Checking for new comments since last commit...')}`);\\n00059|       if (argv.verbose) {\\n00060|         await log(`   PR #${prNumber} on branch: ${branchName}`, { verbose: true });\\n00061|         await log(`   Owner/Repo: ${owner}/${repo}`, { verbose: true });\\n00062|         if (repositoryPath) {\\n00063|           await log(`   Repository path: ${repositoryPath}`, { verbose: true });\\n00064|         }\\n00065|       }\\n00066| \\n00067|       // Get the last commit timestamp from the PR branch\\n00068|       let lastCommitTime = null;\\n00069|       // Issue #2130: quiet. These probes answer \\\"when was the last commit?\\\", and\\n00070|       // the answer is logged in words below; mirroring them put bare ISO dates\\n00071|       // and `git log`'s \\\"unknown revision\\\" complaint - the expected outcome for\\n00072|       // a branch that has never been pushed - into the attached log.\\n00073|       const git$ = repositoryPath ? $({ cwd: repositoryPath, ...QUIET_PROBE }) : quietProbe($);\\n00074|       let lastCommitResult = await git$`git log -1 --format=\\\"%aI\\\" origin/${branchName}`;\\n00075|       if (lastCommitResult.code !== 0) {\\n00076|         // Fallback to local branch if remote doesn't exist\\n00077|         lastCommitResult = await git$`git log -1 --format=\\\"%aI\\\" ${branchName}`;\\n00078|       }\\n00079| \\n00080|       if (lastCommitResult.code === 0) {\\n00081|         lastCommitTime = new Date(lastCommitResult.stdout.toString().trim());\\n00082|         await log(formatAligned('📅', 'Last commit time:', lastCommitTime.toISOString(), 2));\\n00083|       } else {\\n00084|         // Fallback: Get last commit time from GitHub API\\n00085|         try {\\n00086|           const prCommitsResult = await quietProbe($)`gh api repos/${owner}/${repo}/pulls/${prNumber}/commits --paginate --jq 'last.commit.author.date'`;\\n00087|           if (prCommitsResult.code === 0 && prCommitsResult.stdout?.toString()) {\\n00088|             lastCommitTime = new Date(prCommitsResult.stdout.toString().trim());\\n00089|             await log(formatAligned('📅', 'Last commit time (from API):', lastCommitTime.toISOString(), 2));\\n00090|           }\\n00091|         } catch (error) {\\n00092|           reportError(error, {\\n00093|             context: 'get_last_commit_time',\\n00094|             prNumber,\\n00095|             operation: 'fetch_commit_timestamp',\\n00096|           });\\n00097|           await log(`Warning: Could not get last commit time: ${cleanErrorMessage(error)}`, { level: 'warning' });\\n00098|         }\\n00099|       }\\n00100| \\n00101|       // Only proceed if we have a last commit time\\n00102|       if (lastCommitTime) {\\n00103|         // Define log patterns to filter out comments containing logs from solve.mjs\\n00104|         const logPatterns = [/📊.*Log file|solution\\\\s+draft.*log/i, /🔗.*Link:|💻.*Session:/i, /Generated with.*solve\\\\.mjs/i, /Session ID:|Log file available:/i];\\n00105| \\n00106|         // Issue #1827: A comment is tool-generated if its ID was tracked in\\n00107|         // memory during this run (system status comments AND the agent's own\\n00108|         // session comments) or if its body carries a known tool marker (catches\\n00109|         // comments from previous runs whose IDs are gone). These must never\\n00110|         // count as feedback — otherwise the agent's own \\\"CI now green\\\" / status\\n00111|         // comments trigger an endless restart loop (see PR link-foundation/rust-web-box#34).\\n00112|         const isToolComment = comment => isToolTrackedCommentId(comment.id) || isToolGeneratedComment(comment.body);\\n00113| \\n00114|         // Count new PR comments after last commit (both code review comments and conversation comments)\\n00115|         let prReviewComments = [];\\n00116|         let prConversationComments = [];\\n00117| \\n00118|         // Get PR code review comments (use --paginate to get all comments, not just first page)\\n00119|         const prReviewCommentsResult = await quietProbe($)`gh api repos/${owner}/${repo}/pulls/${prNumber}/comments --paginate`;\\n00120|         if (prReviewCommentsResult.code === 0) {\\n00121|           prReviewComments = JSON.parse(prReviewCommentsResult.stdout.toString());\\n00122|         }\\n00123| \\n00124|         // Get PR conversation comments (PR is also an issue)\\n00125|         // Use --paginate to get all comments - GitHub API returns max 30 per page by default\\n00126|         const prConversationCommentsResult = await quietProbe($)`gh api repos/${owner}/${repo}/issues/${prNumber}/comments --paginate`;\\n00127|         if (prConversationCommentsResult.code === 0) {\\n00128|           prConversationComments = JSON.parse(prConversationCommentsResult.stdout.toString());\\n00129|         }\\n00130| \\n00131|         // Helper function to filter comments based on time and log patterns\\n00132|         const filterComment = comment => {\\n00133|           // Issue #1827: never count tool-generated comments as feedback.\\n00134|           if (isToolComment(comment)) {\\n00135|             return false;\\n00136|           }\\n00137|           const commentTime = new Date(comment.created_at);\\n00138|           const isAfterCommit = commentTime > lastCommitTime;\\n00139|           const isNotLogPattern = !logPatterns.some(pattern => pattern.test(comment.body || ''));\\n00140| \\n00141|           // If we have a work start time and current user, filter out comments made by claude tool after work started\\n00142|           if (workStartTime && currentUser && comment.user && comment.user.login === currentUser) {\\n00143|             const isAfterWorkStart = commentTime > new Date(workStartTime);\\n00144|             if (isAfterWorkStart && argv.verbose) {\\n00145|               // Note: Filtering out own comment from user after work started\\n00146|             }\\n00147|             return isAfterCommit && !isAfterWorkStart && isNotLogPattern;\\n00148|           }\\n00149| \\n00150|           return isAfterCommit && isNotLogPattern;\\n00151|         };\\n00152| \\n00153|         // Filter and count PR review comments (inline code comments) separately\\n00154|         const filteredPrReviewComments = prReviewComments.filter(filterComment);\\n00155|         newPrReviewComments = filteredPrReviewComments.length;\\n00156| \\n00157|         // Filter and count PR conversation comments separately\\n00158|         const filteredPrConversationComments = prConversationComments.filter(filterComment);\\n00159|         newPrComments = filteredPrConversationComments.length;\\n00160| \\n00161|         // Combined count for logging purposes\\n00162|         const allPrComments = [...prReviewComments, ...prConversationComments];\\n00163| \\n00164|         // Count new issue comments after last commit\\n00165|         // Use --paginate to get all comments - GitHub API returns max 30 per page by default\\n00166|         const issueCommentsResult = await quietProbe($)`gh api repos/${owner}/${repo}/issues/${issueNumber}/comments --paginate`;\\n00167|         if (issueCommentsResult.code === 0) {\\n00168|           const issueComments = JSON.parse(issueCommentsResult.stdout.toString());\\n00169|           const filteredIssueComments = issueComments.filter(comment => {\\n00170|             // Issue #1827: never count tool-generated comments as feedback.\\n00171|             if (isToolComment(comment)) {\\n00172|               return false;\\n00173|             }\\n00174|             const commentTime = new Date(comment.created_at);\\n00175|             const isAfterCommit = commentTime > lastCommitTime;\\n00176|             const isNotLogPattern = !logPatterns.some(pattern => pattern.test(comment.body || ''));\\n00177| \\n00178|             // If we have a work start time and current user, filter out comments made by claude tool after work started\\n00179|             if (workStartTime && currentUser && comment.user && comment.user.login === currentUser) {\\n00180|               const isAfterWorkStart = commentTime > new Date(workStartTime);\\n00181|               if (isAfterWorkStart && argv.verbose) {\\n00182|                 // Note: Filtering out own issue comment from user after work started\\n00183|               }\\n00184|               return isAfterCommit && !isAfterWorkStart && isNotLogPattern;\\n00185|             }\\n00186| \\n00187|             return isAfterCommit && isNotLogPattern;\\n00188|           });\\n00189|           newIssueComments = filteredIssueComments.length;\\n00190|         }\\n00191| \\n00192|         await log(formatAligned('💬', 'New PR comments:', newPrComments.toString(), 2));\\n00193|         await log(formatAligned('💬', 'New PR review comments:', newPrReviewComments.toString(), 2));\\n00194|         await log(formatAligned('💬', 'New issue comments:', newIssueComments.toString(), 2));\\n00195| \\n00196|         if (argv.verbose) {\\n00197|           await log(`   Total new comments: ${newPrComments + newPrReviewComments + newIssueComments}`, { verbose: true });\\n00198|           await log(`   Comment lines to add: ${newPrComments > 0 || newPrReviewComments > 0 || newIssueComments > 0 ? 'Yes' : 'No (saving tokens)'}`, { verbose: true });\\n00199|           await log(`   PR review comments fetched: ${prReviewComments.length}`, { verbose: true });\\n00200|           await log(`   PR conversation comments fetched: ${prConversationComments.length}`, { verbose: true });\\n00201|           await log(`   Total PR comments checked: ${allPrComments.length}`, { verbose: true });\\n00202|         }\\n00203| \\n00204|         // Check if --auto-continue-only-on-new-comments is enabled and fail if no new comments\\n00205|         if (argv.autoContinueOnlyOnNewComments && (isContinueMode || argv.autoContinue)) {\\n00206|           const totalNewComments = newPrComments + newPrReviewComments + newIssueComments;\\n00207|           if (totalNewComments === 0) {\\n00208|             await log('❌ auto-continue-only-on-new-comments: No new comments found since last commit');\\n00209|             await log('   This option requires new comments to proceed with auto-continue or continue mode.');\\n00210|             process.exit(1);\\n00211|           } else {\\n00212|             await log(`✅ auto-continue-only-on-new-comments: Found ${totalNewComments} new comments, continuing...`);\\n00213|           }\\n00214|         }\\n00215| \\n00216|         // Build comprehensive feedback info for system prompt\\n00217|         feedbackLines = []; // Reset for this execution\\n00218|         let feedbackDetected = false;\\n00219|         const feedbackSources = [];\\n00220| \\n00221|         // Add comment info if counts are > 0 to avoid wasting tokens\\n00222|         if (newPrComments > 0) {\\n00223|           feedbackLines.push(promptText('prompt.templates.solve-feedback.detectAndCountFeedback.text1', { newPrComments: String(newPrComments) }));\\n00224|         }\\n00225|         if (newPrReviewComments > 0) {\\n00226|           feedbackLines.push(promptText('prompt.templates.solve-feedback.detectAndCountFeedback.text2', { newPrReviewComments: String(newPrReviewComments) }));\\n00227|         }\\n00228|         if (newIssueComments > 0) {\\n00229|           feedbackLines.push(promptText('prompt.templates.solve-feedback.detectAndCountFeedback.text3', { newIssueComments: String(newIssueComments) }));\\n00230|         }\\n00231| \\n00232|         // Enhanced feedback detection for all continue modes\\n00233|         if (isContinueMode || argv.autoContinue) {\\n00234|           if (argv.continueOnlyOnFeedback) {\\n00235|             await log(`${formatAligned('🔍', 'Feedback detection:', 'Checking for any feedback since last commit...')}`);\\n00236|           }\\n00237| \\n00238|           // 1. Check for new comments (already filtered above)\\n00239|           const totalNewComments = newPrComments + newPrReviewComments + newIssueComments;\\n00240|           if (totalNewComments > 0) {\\n00241|             feedbackDetected = true;\\n00242|             feedbackSources.push(`New comments (${totalNewComments})`);\\n00243|           }\\n00244| \\n00245|           // 2. Check for edited titles/descriptions\\n00246|           // Issue #895: edits made during the current work session are the agent's own.\\n00247|           // Issue #2293: `updated_at` is bumped by comments, labels, cross-references and\\n00248|           // pull request creation, and most real edits after the last commit were made by\\n00249|           // the previous solver session (same account). Read the actual edit history,\\n00250|           // ignore edits made inside the solver's own sessions, and report evidence.\\n00251|           try {\\n00252|             const editTargets = [{ kind: 'pr', number: prNumber, label: 'Pull request', source: 'PR description edited' }];\\n00253|        